# Authentication Skill

## Stack

* JWT Authentication
* Refresh Tokens
* OAuth2PasswordBearer
* FastAPI Security
* bcrypt password hashing

## Authentication Architecture

Use layered auth structure:

```
auth/
 ├── routers/
 ├── services/
 ├── repositories/
 ├── schemas/
 └── utils/
```

## Authentication Rules

* Use JWT access tokens
* Use refresh tokens
* Hash passwords using bcrypt
* Never store plain passwords
* Never expose tokens in logs

## Password Rules

* Hash all passwords
* Never return password hashes
* Enforce minimum password length
* Validate password strength

## Token Rules

* Access tokens should be short-lived
* Refresh tokens should be revocable
* Validate token expiration
* Store secrets in environment variables

## Authorization Rules

* Protect private routes
* Use role-based access control when needed
* Validate user permissions before actions

## FastAPI Rules

* Use Depends for auth dependencies
* Use OAuth2PasswordBearer
* Create reusable auth dependencies

## Frontend Rules

* Store tokens securely
* Never hardcode tokens
* Handle token expiration
* Redirect unauthorized users properly

## Security Rules

* Never expose secret keys
* Never trust frontend authentication state
* Validate user identity server-side
* Rate limit login endpoints
* Prevent brute force attacks

## API Rules

Authentication endpoints:

```txt id="jlwm9t"
POST /auth/login
POST /auth/register
POST /auth/refresh
POST /auth/logout
GET /auth/me
```

## Response Rules

Successful login response:

```
{
  "success": true,
  "accessToken": "jwt-token",
  "refreshToken": "refresh-token",
  "user": {}
}
```

## Error Handling

* Return proper HTTP status codes
* Never reveal whether email exists
* Return generic auth failure messages

## User Model Rules

User model should include:

* id
* email
* hashed_password
* role
* is_active
* created_at

## Recommended Flow

```
Frontend Login
 -> Backend Validation
 -> Password Verification
 -> Generate JWT
 -> Return Tokens
```

## Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

* Storing plain passwords
* Long-lived access tokens
* Hardcoded secrets
* Returning sensitive user fields
* Trusting frontend roles
* Putting auth logic inside routers
